# Overview #overview

This document is intentionally shaped like a small technical manual. The left navigation is built from first-level headings and acts as macro navigation. The right navigation lists second- and third-level headings for section-level movement.

## When to use docs layout #when-to-use-docs

Use the docs layout when the document is longer than a single article and readers need stable entry points. Product guides, implementation notes, SDK references, and internal runbooks are good candidates.

### Reader expectations #reader-expectations

Readers expect the main content to stay focused while navigation stays visible. Headings should be short, stable, and written as destinations.

### Source expectations #source-expectations

The source remains a normal NODX document. There is no separate route file, no custom sidebar schema, and no hidden build step for navigation.

## Navigation model #navigation-model

First-level headings define the primary route map. Second- and third-level headings define local structure. Deeper headings can still render in the page, but should be used sparingly.

| Level | Purpose | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| H1 | Macro navigation | Overview |
| H2 | Section navigation | Navigation model |
| H3 | Local detail | Reader expectations |

# Authoring #authoring

Docs pages should be easy to edit as text. The preview adds a shell, but the document should still make sense in a terminal, code review, or semantic projection.

## Front matter #front-matter

The docs shell is selected with `layout: docs` or `theme: docs`. `layout` controls structure. `theme` controls visual defaults. Using both makes the intent explicit.

### Minimal setup #minimal-setup

```nodx
---
title: My Handbook
theme: docs
layout: docs
---
```

## Heading discipline #heading-discipline

Prefer a small number of H1 sections. Each H1 should be a major destination. If the left navigation becomes noisy, the document probably needs fewer top-level sections.

::note {type="tip"}
Use stable heading IDs for documentation that will be linked from issues, pull requests, changelogs, or support threads.
::note

## Inline content #inline-content

Inline formatting, links, variables, references, and citations work the same way inside the docs layout as they do in the web theme.

# Rendering #rendering

The complete HTML renderer emits the full documentation shell. Hosts that render fragments need to reproduce the same shell if they want an accurate preview.

## Shell regions #shell-regions

The rendered page has three regions: sidebar, main content, and local outline. The playground mirrors this structure so authors can inspect the real layout contract.

### Sidebar behavior #sidebar-behavior

The sidebar uses only H1 entries. Those links move between major sections.

### Outline behavior #outline-behavior

The right outline uses H2 and H3 entries. Those links move within and across the current document section.

## Export behavior #export-behavior

Exported HTML should preserve the docs shell when `layout: docs` is present. Switching the theme selector in the playground changes visual tokens, but the document metadata remains the source of truth.

# Integration #integration

Applications can embed the renderer directly, serve exported HTML, or use NODX as a source format in a documentation pipeline.

## Playground host #playground-host

The playground is a host application. It loads `.nodx` files, renders them, exposes AST/NCP/semantic inspectors, and resolves package-local assets without extracting files to disk.

### Package assets #package-assets

Stored ZIP packages can include styles, assets, component manifests, and the entry document. The Package tab shows what the host loaded.

## Remote policy #remote-policy

Remote styles or components should be fetched by the host, checked against policy, and passed to the renderer as trusted input. The renderer itself should not fetch arbitrary network resources.

# Operations #operations

Documentation is only useful if it can be validated and maintained over time.

## Validation #validation

Run validation in CI so broken anchors, unsafe URLs, invalid styles, and unsupported capabilities are caught before publication.

## Review workflow #review-workflow

Because the source is plain text, reviewers can discuss content changes in normal code review. The generated navigation follows the headings, so navigation changes are visible in the diff.

## Publishing #publishing

Static hosting is enough for this playground and for exported docs. GitHub Pages can serve the documentation site and the browser playground from the same artifact.
